          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em continuum
            is in general something opposite to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+object"} discrete
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Often
                %strong the continuum
                is taken to refer to the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
                %em cardinality of the continuum
                referse to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum+hypothesis"} continuum hypothesis
                in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong metric continuum
                is any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+space"} connected
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metric space
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+topology"} general topology
                %strong continuum
                is taken to mean a compact
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axiom"} Hausdorff
                connected space.
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            one may also mean by
            %em a continuum
            a medium which spreads the physical quantities spatially with some finite density, unlike the physics of a system of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            = succeed "-like)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delta-distribution"} delta-distribution
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics"} geometry of physics
            for more on the relation of the continuum in form of the real line to physics.
          %h2#InCohesiveHomotopyTypeTheory In cohesive homotopy type theory
          %p
            One can
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatize
            aspects of the notion of
            %em line continuum
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1
            all whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+element"} points
            are, while different, connectable by
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+groupoid"} paths
            (and uniquely so, up to suitable
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+infinity-groupoid+in+a+locally+infinity-connected+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} fundamental ∞-groupoid functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Pi}
            to it, the result is something
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-1%29-connected"} contractible
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔸
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo *
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{\Pi}(\mathbb{A}^1) \simeq *
                  \\,.
          #RealLineExhibitsSmoothCohesion.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              In the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
              called
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Smooth%E2%88%9EGrpd"} Smooth∞Grpd
              we have a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful
              embedding of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
              as well as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              structure. This is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+object"} discrete
              for the first two, but not for the last one, and homotopy cohesion can detect this:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ;
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbf{\Pi}(\mathbb{Z}) \simeq \mathbb{Z}
                    \\,;
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ;
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbf{\Pi}(\mathbb{Q}) \simeq \mathbb{Q}
                    \\,;
            %p but
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo *
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbf{\Pi}(\mathbb{R}) \simeq *
                    \\,.
            %p
              This reflects the fact that the points of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              form a continuum, but those of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
              do not.
            %p
              Also the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
              with their canonical manifold structure of course form a continuum in this sense
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo *
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbf{\Pi}(\mathbb{C}) \simeq *
                    \\,.
            %p
              But the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
              in fact “exhibits the cohesion” (in the sense of
              %a{:href => "cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos#A1ExhibitingCohesion"} this definition
              by
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos+--+structures#RealLineIsTheContinuum"} this discussion
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
              is equivalent to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization+of+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} localization
              at
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-) \times \mathbb{R}^1 \longrightarrow (-)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Pi \simeq L_{\mathbb{R}^1}
                    \\,.
            %p
              This is not true in smooth cohesion for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^1
              replaced by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^1
              and in this sense
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^1
              is “the real continuum” here (in both sense of the word “real”).
          #ContractibilityOfAFromThatOfI.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              For a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+object"} ring object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔸
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1
              to be geometrically contractible,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝔸
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(\mathbb{A}^1) \simeq *
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              from a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bi-pointed+object"} bi-pointed object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi left
                    %mo ,
                    %mi right
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (left, right, I)
              to the bipointed object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔸
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0, 1, \mathbb{A}^1)
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              is geometrically contractible.
            %p
              Hence in words: “If in the ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔸
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1
              the elements 0 and 1 are path-connected, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝔸
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1
              is already contractible, hence is a line continuum.”
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Under the given assumptions we obtain a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
              of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi 𝔸
                              %mn 1
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ↘
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mn 0
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi I
                            %mo ×
                            %msup
                              %mi 𝔸
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi mult
                                %mo ∘
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi i
                                %mo ,
                                %mi id
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi 𝔸
                              %mn 1
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↗
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi id
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi 𝔸
                              %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\mathbb{A}^1
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{ 0}}
                    \\\
                    I \times \mathbb{A}^1 &amp;\stackrel{ mult\circ (i,id)}{\to}&amp; \mathbb{A}^1
                    \\\
                    \\uparrow &amp; \nearrow_{\mathrlap{id}}
                    \\\
                    \\mathbb{A}^1
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
              preserves products, it sends this to a diagram of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                            %msup
                              %mo ↘
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mn 0
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Π
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi 𝔸
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ⇓
                              %mo ≃
                          %mtd
                            %mi Π
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi 𝔸
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                            %msub
                              %mo ↗
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi id
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp; \nearrow \searrow^{\mathrlap{0}}
                    \\\
                    \\Pi(\mathbb{A}^1) &amp;\Downarrow_{\simeq}&amp; \Pi(\mathbb{A}^1)
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \searrow \nearrow_{\mathrlap{id}}
                    }
                    \\,,
            %p
              which exhibits a contracting
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝔸
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(\mathbb{A}^1)
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              The standard unit intervals
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1] \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R} \in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TopMfd"} TopMfd
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ↪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hookrightarrow
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETop%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ETop∞Grpd
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1] \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R} \in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmthMfd"} SmthMfd
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ↪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hookrightarrow
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Smooth%E2%88%9EGrpd"} Smooth∞Grpd
              satisfy the assumptions of prop.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ContractibilityOfAFromThatOfI"}
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              of prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ContractibilityOfAFromThatOfI"}
              is in general not an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+type"} interval type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(I)
              is. See the discussion
              %em
                %a{:href => "cohesive%20homotopy%20type%20theory#GeometricSpacesAndTheirHomotopyTypes"} Geometric spaces and their cohesive homotopy types
              at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
              for more on this.
          %h2#References References
          %ul
            %li#Johnstone08
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
              ,
              %em Topos-theoretic models of the continuum
              2008 (
              %a{:href => "http://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/quantum/slides/clap2-peterjohnstone.pdf"}> slides
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?feature=player_embedded&v=pKWYa9sc5UI"} lecture video
        .revisedby
